    c103: '"Unsorted lists are no use to anyone. Every item and its price, most expensive first."', c104: '"Now cut the list short. The five items we have the most of."', c105: '"The same city is on lots of rows. I want each city once."',
    c106: '"You can work out a column as well as read one. Price minus cost for every item. Call it margin."', c107: '"Sort by two things. Region first, then the biggest credit limits inside each region."', c108: '"Dates sort like anything else. The three most recently hired."', c109: '"Put it all together. The ten most valuable piles of stock."',
    c201: '"WHERE keeps only the rows that match. Keep the Gold accounts."', c202: '"You can compare two columns on the same row. Which items are below their reorder level?"', c203: '"Two conditions, both true. Silver accounts on the coast."', c204: '"Either of two. Customers in one harbor or the other."',
    c205: '"A range. Items priced in the middle, not cheap and not dear."', c206: '"Match part of a word. Everything with Iron in its name."', c207: '"Some values are empty. Find the accounts with no factor."', c209: '"AND and OR together. Use brackets; this one catches everybody once."', c210: '"Put it together. The spring orders, leaving out anything cancelled."',
    c301: '"The Third Circle turns lots of rows into one number. Start with: how many customers are there?"', c302: '"SUM instead of COUNT. What’s all our stock worth, at what we paid for it?"', c303: '"Three numbers in one answer: cheapest, most expensive, and the average, in that order."', c304: '"One number for the whole table is a start. Now one number for each city."',
    c305: '"Two numbers for each group: how much of each kind, and its average price."', c306: '"Group the orders by status."', c307: '"HAVING keeps only some of the groups. Accounts with fourteen orders or more."', c308: '"WHERE filters rows first, HAVING filters groups after. Shipped orders only: which factors have shipped more than fifty?"', c309: '"COUNT skips empty values. Three counts in one row, so you can see how they differ."',
    c401: '"An order only has a customer number. JOIN the customers table to get the name."', c402: '"Join items to the vendors who make them. Who supplies the potions?"', c403: '"One order, line by line, with the item name next to each line."', c404: '"Three tables in one answer. Each cancelled order, its customer and its factor."',
    c405: '"Join, then total. The ten best customers by shipped revenue."', c406: '"The same total, split by what was sold instead of who bought it."', c407: '"A plain JOIN drops rows with no match. Count orders for every customer, including the ones with none. That’s a LEFT JOIN."', c408: '"Now only the rows with no match. The accounts that never ordered."',
    c410: '"A table can join to itself. Each employee next to their manager."', c411: '"Two joins from one table can multiply your rows. Per vendor: items supplied and purchase orders sent. Check your counts."',
